You do not need to become a “quant” in the scary sense to use this well. For your use case, TA is really just a way to describe:

how unusual the drop was
whether selling looked exhausted
whether price is stretched relative to its recent behavior
whether the stock is stabilizing

That is very learnable.

The right mindset for TA in your project

You should not think:

“Can RSI predict stocks?”

You should think:

“Does this technical condition help me estimate whether a +1% bounce within 14 days is more likely?”

That makes it much less intimidating.

You are not trying to invent some fancy hedge fund model.
You are just asking whether a few interpretable signals improve your bounce-quality framework.

TA is probably useful in 3 ways
1. Measuring how stretched the move is

A raw -6% day means different things for different stocks.

Useful indicators here:

move relative to ATR
return z-score vs recent history
distance below moving averages
RSI

These help tell you whether a move is genuinely extreme.

2. Detecting exhaustion versus true breakdown

Two stocks can both be down 7%, but:

one looks like panic-selling exhaustion
one looks like the beginning of a downtrend

Useful signals:

volume spike
close position within the day’s range
whether price reclaimed some of the drop by close
whether it broke major recent support
whether trend was already weak before the event
3. Improving entry timing

Even if you keep the same universe, TA may tell you:

enter on the drop day close
or wait one day for stabilization
or avoid names still in freefall

That could be huge.

TA concepts that are actually worth learning for you

If I had to teach you only a small set, I’d pick these.

ATR

Average True Range.
This measures typical daily movement.

Why it matters:

a 4% drop in a stock with 2% normal daily movement is very different from a 4% drop in a stock with 6% normal movement

This is probably one of the most useful things for your project.

Think of:

today_drop / ATR

That gives you a normalized shock size.

RSI

Relative Strength Index.
This tells you whether recent price action has been heavily skewed toward selling or buying.

People abuse it, but for your project it can be useful as:

a rough “how oversold is this” feature

Not as a trading rule by itself.

Moving averages

Like 10-day, 20-day, 50-day moving averages.

Useful for:

identifying whether the stock was already trending down
measuring how stretched price is below recent average

For example:

price 8% below 20-day MA after a one-day panic move may matter
Volume spike

A big red day on huge volume can mean:

capitulation
or strong informed selling

This is not obvious a priori, which is exactly why it’s a good feature to test.

Close location in range

This one is underrated and very intuitive.

If a stock had a horrible day but closed near the high of the day, that suggests buyers stepped in.
If it closed near the low of the day, selling pressure may still be active.

This could be very relevant for bounce probability.

TA features I think are most worth testing for your specific goal

If your goal is:

long only
take +1%
preferably quickly
max hope around 14 days

then the best TA features are probably:

drop size relative to ATR
RSI at event day close
distance from 20-day MA
volume spike relative to average
close position in day range
prior 5-day trend
prior 20-day trend

That’s already plenty.

TA features I would avoid at first

Do not get dragged into:

MACD crossovers
Fibonacci retracements
Elliott wave nonsense
dozens of candlestick patterns
overly complicated indicator soup

That is where it starts feeling fake and overwhelming.

You want:

simple
interpretable
testable
The big question TA can help answer

The key thing TA might improve is not:

“should I buy this stock?”

It is:

“among all drop events, which ones are most likely to produce a clean bounce soon?”

That fits your framework perfectly.

How I’d mentally organize the full future model

You can think of the eventual bounce score as having 3 buckets of information:

1. Price shock features
percent drop
ATR-adjusted drop
relative to market/sector
2. Technical condition features
RSI
moving average stretch
volume spike
close in range
prior trend
3. Event/news features
earnings miss
analyst downgrade
catastrophic / too bad to touch
macro sympathy move
no obvious news

That is actually a very nice system design.
And notice: you don’t need deep quant math to reason about any of those.

What should scare you versus not scare you

What should not scare you:

computing indicators
testing historical hit rates
using TA as features
building simple scores

What should scare you a bit:

overfitting
adding too many indicators
believing a signal before testing it
mistaking explanation for edge

So your coder mindset is actually an advantage.
A coder says:

define feature
compute feature
backtest feature
keep only what helps

That is exactly the right attitude.

My honest take

I think some technical analysis is very likely worth learning for this project.
Not because TA is magic, but because it gives you structured ways to quantify:

oversoldness
abnormality
exhaustion
stabilization

And those are directly relevant to short-horizon mean reversion.

So yes, I would include TA, but in a very constrained way.

If I were teaching you this from scratch

I would teach it in this order:

ATR
RSI
moving averages and stretch
volume spike
close-in-range / candle structure
prior trend

That order gives you maximum usefulness with minimum nonsense.

